  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM EDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\n- Slight chance for rain this weekend\n\nCurrent radar this morning shows isolated and mostly light elevated \nshowers crossing Lake MI and entering Lower MI. Upstream VWPs such \nas KMKX/TMKE indicate strong lower tropospheric veering indicative \nof forced isentropic ascent. The 31/00Z DTX sounding showed a dry \nlower troposphere; this should result in spotty precipitation \namounts around a trace for Lower Michigan into today. Trick or \ntreating tonight does not appear at risk of a washout.\n\nSunday looks similar in terms of hit or miss light rain. A weak and \npoorly phased upper trough and attendant PV max will cross Lower MI. \nIt's not out of the question that a better coverage of rain will \nresult, but ensemble guidance such as the ECE has been trending \ndrier and this may speak to the continued dry lower tropospheric \nconditions still expected.\n\n\n- Warmer and wetter by mid-week\n\nGOES-W satellite shows a phasing and meridionally deep upper trough \noff the west coast about to move onshore. It will cross the Rockies \nwhen a well developed Bermuda High allows increasingly favorable \nmoisture transport from the Gulf. The moist transport will be \naugmented by troughing in the lee of the Rockies in response to the \napproach of the upper trough. Consequently, we will be looking at \nseasonably very large precipitable water values by Monday night,\nwith ECE mean values reaching or exceeding the 99th percentile\nrelative to climatology.\n\nNot surprisingly, precipitation chances go up considerably around \nthis time thanks to the increased moisture and forcing for ascent.\nPrecipitation efficiency will be good but there is some question \nabout total amounts given that the trough will be fairly \nprogressive. Nonetheless, this is normally when fall leaves and \nstorm drains do their annual clogging dance. Thus, nuisance \nflooding is certainly possible from quick rainfall accumulations \nof even an hour or less. Severe weather is not expected and there \nis some question about whether there will be enough conditional \ninstability for any thunderstorms in the first place.\n\nWe still expect anomalously warm temperatures early this week. To \nillustrate, an expected high of 70 at Grand Rapids on Tuesday would \nbe about 15 degrees above normal.\n\n\n- Limited forecast certainty from mid-week onward\n\nStarting in the Wednesday timeframe, the previously mentioned \norganized and phased upper trough appears to weaken while at the \nsame time there is background unphasing of the northern and \nsouthern streams. There is a wide variety of possible forecast \nscenarios during this transition. One tricky possibility would be \nthe development of a cutoff low to focus precipitation chances. \nHowever, predictability for a targeted and relatively impactful \noutcome such as this is very poor, so the best compromise at this \npoint is keeping PoPs in the 20 to 30 percent range for the latter\npart of the week.",
